   "k": "1mz1ygs",
   "en": "victory points"
  },
  {
   "k": "rc1af9",
   "en": "Worst case scenario is that the UK is involved in WW2. Capitulate them and activate the collaboration government before ⟦0⟧ falls to keep British land.",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
   "k": "y6f35m",
   "en": "France"
  },
  {
   "k": "teori7",
   "en": "Allies"
  },
  {
   "k": "14yu885",
   "en": "If you want to join the Allies it's advisable to focus on your naval and air capacity. You'll struggle to build a large ground force with low manpower so you'll mostly be restricted to support roles. 16 divisions should be enough to protect your territory, as once France and Norway have fallen the Germans will try to invade as you're a softer target than the UK. Build up some coastal forts and AA to protect yourself as if they take Dublin you're going to struggle to kick them out again."
  },
  {
   "k": "btiqoi",
   "en": "Build your navy around subs, destroyers and use heavy cruisers as your capital ships, you won't have the capacity to build battleships or carriers. Your air force should be comprised of fighters and naval bombers to dominate your waters, you won't need close air support until quite late in the war."
  },
  {
   "k": "36lu8h",
   "en": "Most of your time should be spent on protecting and raiding convoys in the waters on Northern Europe, through air superiority and mobile raiding fleets you can decimate Axis supply to Norway and pave the way for Allied naval invasions into France. Stay away from large Axis fleets, the Royal Navy can handle them while you mop up smaller forces."
  },
  {
   "k": "xznj9y",
   "en": "Once the time comes for D-Day you can send attachments of troops and CAS to support the Allied push, move wisely and carefully as manpower will still be an issue and you can't afford to throw units into the meat grinder."
  },
